 publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Synek</surname><given-names>VM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Value of a revised EEG coma scale for prognosis after cerebral anoxia and diffuse head injury</article-title><source>Clin Electroencephalogr</source><year>1990</year><volume>21</volume><fpage>25</fpage><lpage>30</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2297945</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B28"><label>28</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Uldry</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name><name><surname>Despland</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name><name><surname>Regli</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>[Modifications in and prognostic value of the EEG in anoxic encephalopathy: 75 cases]</article-title><source>Schweiz Med Wochenschr</source><year>1991</year><volume>121</volume><fpage>1453</fpage><lpage>1459</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1925477</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Correlation analysis between the EEG grade and GOS (A), GCS and GOS (B). (A) Spearman correlation coefficient=-0.353, <italic>p</italic>&#x0003c;0.01, (B) Spearman correlation coefficient=0.464, <italic>p</italic>&#x0003c;0.01. GCS, Glasgow Coma Scale; GOS, Glasgow Outcome Scale; EEG, electroencephalography.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-52-933-g001"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>The Glasgow Coma Scale</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-52-933-i001"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Etiology and Mental Status</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-52-933-i002"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>EEG Grade and GOS</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-52-933-i003"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>GOS, Glasgow Outcome Scale; EEG, electroencephalography.</p><p>Grade I, good recovery (able to return to work or school); Grade II, moderate disability (able to live independently; unable to return to work or school); Grade III, severe disability (able to follow commands; unable to live independently); Grade IV, vegetative state (unable to interact with environment; unresponsive); Grade V, death. EEG Grade I, dominant; normal alpha activity with theta-delta activities; EEG Grade II, dominant theta-delta activity with still detectable normal alpha activities; EEG Grade III, theta-delta activity without alpha activities; EEG Grade IV, delta activity, low voltage, possibly with short isoelectric intervals or dominant, monomorphic, nonreactive alpha activity (alpha coma), or periodic generalized phenomena (spikes, sharp waves, slow waves) with very low-voltage background activity, EEG Grade V, very flat to isoelectric EEG (less than 10-20 mV).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>